        <h2 id="trains">Trains from Hersham</h2>
        <p>Hersham is served by South Western Railway stopping trains on the South West Main Line, between London Waterloo and Woking. It has two platforms, both alongside the outer local lines; the two fast lines through the middle of the station have no platforms, so non-stopping trains pass straight through.</p>

        <p>The pattern is simple and easy to remember: two trains an hour in each direction, all day, all stopping services. There is no fast train from Hersham and there cannot be one, because the fast lines run through the middle of the station without platforms. Every Waterloo service takes the same 33 minutes.</p>

        <h3>To London Waterloo, from Platform 1</h3>
        <p>London trains leave from <strong>Platform 1</strong>, roughly at 19 and 49 minutes past each hour, and take <strong>33 minutes</strong> to Waterloo, calling at seven stations on the way. Most start back at Woking.</p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>Journey times from Hersham, Platform 1</caption>
            <thead><tr><th>Calling at</th><th>Time from Hersham</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Esher</td><td>3 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Surbiton</td><td>8 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Wimbledon</td><td>16 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Earlsfield</td><td>19 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Clapham Junction</td><td>23 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Vauxhall</td><td>28 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>London Waterloo</td><td>33 minutes</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>Three of those are worth knowing as interchanges. Clapham Junction opens up Victoria, Gatwick Airport and south London; Vauxhall connects with the Victoria line; Wimbledon connects with the District line and London Trams.</p>

        <h3>Towards Woking, from Platform 2</h3>
        <p>Country-bound trains leave from <strong>Platform 2</strong>, roughly at 21 and 51 minutes past each hour, reaching Woking in <strong>17 to 18 minutes</strong> after five stops. Every service we watched terminated at Woking, so journeys further west, to Guildford, Basingstoke, Portsmouth or Southampton, mean changing there.</p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>Journey times from Hersham, Platform 2</caption>
            <thead><tr><th>Calling at</th><th>Time from Hersham</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Walton-on-Thames</td><td>3 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Weybridge</td><td>6 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Byfleet &amp; New Haw</td><td>9 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>West Byfleet</td><td>12 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Woking</td><td>17 minutes</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>Those times and platforms were read off National Rail's live departures on a Wednesday morning in August 2026, and the pattern held across every service on the board. Evenings, Sundays and engineering work can all change it, and platform allocation can be altered on the day, so check the screens at the station and use the <a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/plan-my-journey" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Western Railway journey planner ↗</a> or the <a href="https://www.nationalrail.co.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Rail journey planner ↗</a> for the train you actually want. We have not printed first and last train times, which change too often to be useful here.</p>

        <p>One pointer from the Office of Rail and Road's data that matches the timetable: London Waterloo is comfortably the main destination for journeys from Hersham, accounting for 363,658 of them in 2024/25, roughly half of everything the station handles.</p>

        <h3>Live departures and disruption</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.nationalrail.co.uk/live-trains/departures/hersham/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Live departures from Hersham ↗</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/plan-my-journey" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Western Railway service updates ↗</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.nationalrail.co.uk/status-and-disruptions/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Rail planned engineering work ↗</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Hersham has no station forecourt, so during planned closures rail replacement buses use marked stops on the road rather than a dedicated bay. The onward travel poster marks the rail replacement stop separately from the ordinary bus stops.</p>

        <h2 id="tickets">Tickets, Oyster, contactless and smartcards</h2>
        <p>There is a part-time ticket office and a self-service ticket machine outside the station, near the ticket office and the Platform 2 entrance. The machine has a video help link, so you can speak to someone when the office is shut.</p>

        <h3>Ticket office hours</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Hours</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Monday to Friday</td><td>06:50 to 12:00</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Saturday</td><td>06:50 to 13:40</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Sunday</td><td>08:10 to 15:40</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Those were the published hours in August 2026, and they can change with staff availability or on public holidays. Check <a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/travelling-with-us/at-the-station/hersham" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SWR's Hersham station page ↗</a> for current times.</p>

        <h3>Oyster and contactless</h3>
        <p>Hersham is outside the London fare zones, and neither Oyster nor ordinary contactless tap-in, tap-out travel works here. South Western Railway lists the station as not supporting contactless card validation. You can still pay for a ticket with a contactless bank card at the machine or the office, which is a different thing entirely from tapping at a validator.</p>

        <h3>SWR Touch Smartcard</h3>
        <p>Hersham does issue SWR Touch Smartcards, and tickets can be loaded onto them at either the machine or the ticket office. There are no ticket gates at the station, but you still need a valid ticket before you travel. See <a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/train-tickets/smart-ticketing/touch-smartcard" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SWR's Touch Smartcard page ↗</a> for what can be loaded onto one.</p>

        <h2 id="accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
        <p>Hersham is a <strong>step-free category C</strong> station. In National Rail's own words, there is no step-free access to platforms. Both platforms sit above street level on the railway embankment and are reached by stairs, and the station has no lifts.</p>

        <p>What the station does not have:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Any step-free route to either platform</li>
          <li>Lifts</li>
          <li>An accessible toilet, or any toilet at all</li>
          <li>A station wheelchair</li>
          <li>Full-time staff on hand to help you onto a platform</li>
        </ul>

        <p>What it does have:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A ticket machine with a video help link</li>
          <li>A ramp for boarding the train from the platform</li>
          <li>Help points and departure information on the platforms</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The boarding ramp is worth being clear about, because it is easy to misread as step-free access. It bridges the gap between the platform and the train. It does nothing about the stairs between the street and the platform, which you have to manage first.</p>

        <h3>Passenger assistance</h3>
        <p>South Western Railway lists Hersham as part-time staffed and says assistance is normally provided by the guard on the train rather than by staff at the station. Assistance can be arranged in advance through Passenger Assist, and SWR's published assistance number is <strong>0800 528 2100</strong>. See <a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/travelling-with-us/assisted-travel" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Passenger Assist information ↗</a>.</p>

        <p>If you cannot use stairs, talk to South Western Railway before you travel rather than turning up. <a href="/walton-on-thames-railway-station/">Walton-on-Thames station</a>, the next station towards London, is category B1 with lifts and separate ramped entrances to both operational platforms, and is the nearest genuinely step-free alternative.</p>

        <h2 id="parking">Parking</h2>
        <p>There is no station car park at Hersham. South Western Railway lists car parking as not available, and there are no accessible bays belonging to the station.</p>

        <h3>Walton Park car park</h3>
        <p>The nearest public option is Elmbridge Borough Council's Walton Park car park, which is a separate council facility with its own hours and charges rather than railway parking. In August 2026 the council listed <strong>147 spaces including three accessible bays</strong>, with charges applying Monday to Saturday 08:00 to 22:00 and Sunday 10:00 to 17:00, and no charge on bank and public holidays. Payment is by cash, contactless or RingGo, and Blue Badge holders displaying a valid badge park free.</p>

        <p>Worth saying plainly: those three accessible bays get you into the car park, not onto a platform. The stairs are still the only way up.</p>

        <p>For current tariffs and restrictions, see the <a href="https://www.elmbridge.gov.uk/parking-and-roads/car-parks/find-car-park/walton-park-car-park" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">council's Walton Park car park page ↗</a>. On-street parking near the station is restricted in places, so read the signs rather than assuming. If you need a large dedicated station car park, <a href="/walton-on-thames-railway-station/">Walton-on-Thames station</a> has one.</p>

        <h2>Drop-off, pick-up and taxis</h2>
        <p>Hersham has no station forecourt, so there is no dedicated set-down area and no accessible drop-off point. Drivers need to use a lawful stopping place on the road and keep clear of the bus stops and station entrances.</p>
        <p>There is no taxi rank and no cab office. National Rail's onward travel poster says advance booking is essential and lists local operators' numbers, without endorsing any of them.</p>

        <h2 id="buses">Buses</h2>
        <p>Two bus routes serve the station, from stops A and B on the road outside. Route <strong>564</strong> is the one most people will find useful; route <strong>514</strong> runs so rarely that it is not worth planning around. This table follows National Rail's onward travel poster for Hersham, which was correct at November 2025.</p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Destination</th><th>Routes</th><th>Stop</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Walton Hospital</td><td>564</td><td>A</td></tr>
