        </div>

        <p>The poster states that route 564 operates an hourly daytime service Monday to Saturday, and that route 514 operates a very limited daytime service of one or two buses a day, with no evening or Sunday service on either. Surbiton is also reachable by direct train from Hersham, which will usually beat the 514.</p>

        <p><a href="https://assets.nationalrail.co.uk/e8xgegruud3g/2zK0FSRBKc4QXzGFzxXmQ/6dd5cd5e5926b8bf395c1073e6e2d192/Hersham__HER_.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View the current National Rail onward travel poster for Hersham ↗</a>, which also carries the local area map and the stop letters. For live bus times, check the displays at the stops or contact Traveline.</p>

        <h2>Walking to Hersham village centre</h2>
        <p>The station is not in the village. National Rail's onward travel poster puts it at a <strong>15 to 20 minute walk</strong> to Hersham village centre, and the same again to the Field Common estate, with walking routes marked on the poster's map.</p>
        <p>This is the thing that catches people out about Hersham station: the name suggests the village, but the station sits out towards Walton Park, closer to the boundary between Hersham and Walton-on-Thames. Depending on exactly where you are starting from, <a href="/walton-on-thames-railway-station/">Walton-on-Thames station</a> may suit you better, and it has step-free access and a car park.</p>

        <h2>Cycling</h2>
        <p>There are four cycle storage spaces at Hersham, in unsheltered racks, with CCTV covering the area. That is a genuinely small provision, so do not count on a free space at peak times. South Western Railway restricts full-size bicycles on some peak services; see the <a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/travelling-with-us/travelling-with-a-bike" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SWR bicycle policy ↗</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="facilities">Facilities</h2>
        <h3>Ticketing and information</h3>
        <p>Part-time ticket office, a ticket machine outside with a video help link, smartcard issue and loading, help points and departure information on the platforms, and CCTV. There are no ticket gates.</p>

        <h3>Waiting and refreshments</h3>
        <p>There is no waiting room at Hersham, and no shop, cafe or cash machine. Seating is the one thing the official sources disagree about: South Western Railway lists a seating area at the station, while National Rail's summary lists both seating and a sheltered waiting area as unavailable. Until that is settled on the ground, plan for an open platform and dress for the weather.</p>

        <h3>Toilets</h3>
        <p>None. South Western Railway lists no toilets at Hersham, accessible or otherwise, and no baby-changing facilities.</p>

        <h3>Luggage and lost property</h3>
        <p>There is no left-luggage facility and no lost property office at the station; SWR handles lost property centrally.</p>

        <h2 id="history">History</h2>
        <p>Hersham station is much younger than the railway it stands on. Trains had been running through here since the London and Southampton Railway opened its first stretch, Nine Elms to Woking Common, on 21 May 1838, the same line and the same opening that gave Walton-on-Thames its station. Hersham waited almost another century for one of its own.</p>

        <p>The station opened on <strong>28 September 1936</strong>, built by the Southern Railway. According to <em>The Railway Magazine</em> of November 1936, it opened with cheap day tickets to London on offer, and it was built for the same reason as Berrylands and Byfleet &amp; New Haw on the same stretch of line: new housing. Interwar suburban development around Hersham and Walton Park had produced enough potential passengers to justify a station, and the railway followed the houses rather than the other way round.</p>

        <p>It was built cheaply and quickly, in timber. The two platforms were around 550 feet long, each with a timber booking office at the western end reached by an enclosed staircase, and canopies of about 125 feet with timber valances. Both platforms served the local lines only, with the fast lines running through the middle of the station unplatformed, which is why the station still has the shape it does today, and why the two sides have never been connected to each other other than at street level.</p>

        <p>Electrification followed almost immediately. A trial electric service ran from 1 November 1936, and full public electric services began on 4 July 1937, part of the Southern Railway's wider electrification of the route towards Woking, Guildford and Portsmouth.</p>

        <p>The name has caused local confusion ever since. Walton-on-Thames station was called <em>Walton for Hersham</em> from 1913 and only became Walton-on-Thames in 1935, the year before Hersham got its own station; the two stations sit close to the modern boundary between the two places, and which one is genuinely yours depends on your street rather than your address.</p>

        <h2 id="busy">How busy is Hersham station?</h2>
        <p>The Office of Rail and Road estimated <strong>722,278 passenger entries and exits in 2024/25</strong>, which ranked Hersham 694th out of every station in Great Britain. That figure counts journeys starting or ending here, not individual people.</p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Estimated entries and exits</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>2018/19</td><td>857,684</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2019/20</td><td>809,486</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2020/21</td><td>179,794</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2021/22</td><td>446,966</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2022/23</td><td>594,014</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2023/24</td><td>666,076</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2024/25</td><td>722,278</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>Two things stand out in that run of numbers. The first is how completely the pandemic emptied the station: 2020/21 was under 180,000, around a fifth of the previous year. The second is that the recovery, though steady, is not finished. The 2018/19 figure of 857,684 is the highest in ORR's series, which runs back to 1997/98, and 2024/25 is still roughly 16 per cent below it. Commuting from Hersham has come back, but not all the way.</p>

